PEC】\nL3691: \nL3692: 【401†Ethics and Integrity】\nL3693: \nL3694: ##### Engage with us\nL3695: \nL3696: 【386†Newsroom】\nL3697: \nL3698: 【388†Events】\nL3699: \nL3700: 【402†Networks】\nL3701: ##### Work with us\nL3702: \nL3703: 【403†Careers】\nL3704: \nL3705: 【404†Internships】\nL3706: \nL3707: 【405†Young associates】\nL3708: \nL3709: 【406†Procurement at the OECD】\nL3710: \nL3711: Close menu\nL3712: \nL3713: # Time use database\nL3714: \nL3715: The OECD relies on this database to document how women and men allocate their time over different daily activities.\nL3716: \nL3717: Dataset\nL3718: \nL3719: 【408†Access the Time use database†data-explorer.oecd.org】\nL3720: ## About\nL3721: The OECD Time Use Database provides information on how women and men spend their time. Data are currently available for 30 OECD countries. Differences in survey features, the number of diary days sampled, and the categorisation of activities may all affect the cross-country comparability of results.\nL3722: To improve the comparison of time use across countries, the samples are restricted to populations aged 15-64 and activities are aggregated into five main categories: (1) Unpaid work; (2) Paid work or study; (3) Personal care; (4) Leisure; and (5) Other time use.\nL3723:   * 30 April 2026: New data are available for Bulgaria (new, 2020), Canada (2022), Croatia (new, 2022/23), Germany (2022), Greece (2013/2014, revised), Estonia (2019/21), Finland (2020/21), Mexico (2024), Norway (2022), Poland (2023) and the United States (2024).\nL3724: \nL3725:   * A more detailed breakdown of daily activities is available 【409†here】.\nL3726: \nL3727: \nL3728: \nL3729: Explore our data\nL3730: ## OECD Time Use Database\nL3731: \nL3732: Data on how time is spent on various daily activities\nL3733: \nL3734: 【408†Access data here†data-explorer.oecd.org】\nL3735: ## Related publications\nL3736: \nL3737:   * Policy paper\nL3738: \nL3739: 【410†Caregiving in Crisis: Gender inequality in paid and unpaid work during C